#Instructions for use
Processing is carried out in two consecutive stages. First, the ID script is executed to retrieve sequence identifiers from NCBI Virus. Subsequently, the FASTA script is run; it uses these identifiers to download the CDS regions and retrieve the sequences corresponding to the E7 gene.
Both the target gene and the location can be modified as needed.
1. Run the ID script

The first step involves running the script responsible for querying NCBI Virus and obtaining the accession numbers corresponding to the selected HPV type and geographic location.

Example:

python ID.py

This script:

Queries NCBI Virus using NCBI Datasets.
Downloads records that meet the specified criteria.
Extracts the data_report.jsonl file.
Retrieves the accession numbers from the records.
Removes duplicate accession numbers.
Generates a text file containing the accession numbers.

For example:

HPV16_Argentina/
├── hpv16_Argentina.zip
├── ncbi_descarga/
└── accesiones_HPV16_Argentina.txt

The file:

accesiones_HPV16_Argentina.txt

will be used as input for the second script.

2. Run the FASTA script

Once the ID script has successfully completed, the FASTA script is executed.

Example:

python FASTA.py

This script:

Reads the accession numbers generated by the ID script.
Queries NCBI using E-utilities/EFetch.
Retrieves the CDS regions associated with each accession number.
Searches for the CDS corresponding to the E7 protein/gene.
Extracts the E7 nucleotide sequences.
Saves the identified sequences in FASTA format.
Logs the accession numbers for which E7 was not found. As a result, the following are generated, for example:

HPV58_Brazil/
├── accesiones_HPV58_Brazil.txt
├── HPV58_Brazil_E7.fasta
└── HPV58_Brazil_E7_no_encontradas.txt

The main file of interest is:

HPV58_Brazil_E7.fasta

which contains the E7 nucleotide sequences retrieved from NCBI.
